I'll solve this geometry problem directly in my reasoning, then output the proof.

**Setting up the framework.** Let the circumcircle have radius $R$. Using the inscribed angle theorem, I'll parametrize by four angles, place coordinates with $C$ at the origin, compute the incentre via barycentric coordinates, and verify the collinearity condition reduces to a trigonometric identity.

Let me verify the key identity $\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)\sin(\gamma+\delta) = \sin\delta\sin\epsilon + \sin\gamma\sin(\gamma+\delta+\epsilon)$ via product-to-sum before writing up — confirmed: both sides equal $\tfrac12[\cos(\epsilon-\delta)-\cos(2\gamma+\epsilon+\delta)]$.

---

## Proof

Let the circumcircle of $ABCD$ have radius $R$. Define the inscribed angles
$$\delta=\angle ACB=\angle ADB,\quad \gamma=\angle ACD=\angle ABD,\quad \beta=\angle BAC=\angle BDC,\quad \epsilon=\angle CAD=\angle CBD,$$
each pair subtending the same arc. In $\triangle ABD$ the angles are $\gamma,\delta,\beta+\epsilon$, so
$$\beta+\gamma+\delta+\epsilon=\pi.\tag{1}$$

By the inscribed-angle–chord relation $XY=2R\sin(\text{inscribed angle on }XY)$:
$$CA=2R\sin(\gamma+\epsilon),\quad CB=2R\sin\beta,\quad CD=2R\sin\epsilon,\quad AB=2R\sin\delta,\quad AD=2R\sin\gamma,\quad BD=2R\sin(\beta+\epsilon).$$

Set $\Theta:=\gamma+\delta=\angle BCD$. From (1), $\beta+\epsilon=\pi-\Theta$, hence
$$\sin(\beta+\epsilon)=\sin\Theta,\qquad \sin\beta=\sin(\pi-\Theta-\epsilon)=\sin(\Theta+\epsilon)=\sin(\gamma+\delta+\epsilon).\tag{2}$$

**Coordinates.** Place $C$ at the origin with ray $CB$ along the positive $x$-axis. Since diagonal $CA$ lies inside $\angle BCD$, ray $CA$ makes angle $\delta$ and ray $CD$ makes angle $\Theta$ with the axis. Thus
$$B=(2R\sin\beta,\,0),\quad A=(2R\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)\cos\delta,\;2R\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)\sin\delta),\quad D=(2R\sin\epsilon\cos\Theta,\;2R\sin\epsilon\sin\Theta),$$
and, since $CP=CQ=CA=2R\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)$ with $P\in\overrightarrow{CB}$, $Q\in\overrightarrow{CD}$,
$$P=(2R\sin(\gamma+\epsilon),\,0),\quad Q=(2R\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)\cos\Theta,\;2R\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)\sin\Theta).$$

**Incentre.** The incentre $I$ of $\triangle ABD$ has barycentric coordinates $(BD:DA:AB)=(\sin(\beta+\epsilon):\sin\gamma:\sin\delta)=(\sin\Theta:\sin\gamma:\sin\delta)$. With $S=\sin\Theta+\sin\gamma+\sin\delta$:
$$I=\frac{\sin\Theta\cdot A+\sin\gamma\cdot B+\sin\delta\cdot D}{S}.$$
Writing $r:=2R\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)$, its coordinates are
$$I_x=\frac{2R}{S}\big[\sin\Theta\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)\cos\delta+\sin\gamma\sin\beta+\sin\delta\sin\epsilon\cos\Theta\big],$$
$$I_y=\frac{2R\sin\delta\sin\Theta}{S}\big[\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)+\sin\epsilon\big].$$

**Collinearity test.** A point $(x,y)$ lies on line $PQ$ (whose direction is $(\cos\Theta-1,\sin\Theta)$ up to scale) iff
$$y(\cos\Theta-1)=(x-r)\sin\Theta.\tag{$\star$}$$
Substituting $(I_x,I_y)$ into the left-hand side minus the right-hand side and factoring out $\frac{2R\sin\Theta}{S}$ gives the expression
$$E:=\sin\delta[\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)+\sin\epsilon](\cos\Theta-1)-\big[\sin\Theta\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)\cos\delta+\sin\gamma\sin\beta+\sin\delta\sin\epsilon\cos\Theta\big]+S\sin(\gamma+\epsilon).$$

Expanding $S\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)=(\sin\Theta+\sin\gamma+\sin\delta)\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)$, the terms $\pm\sin\delta\sin\epsilon\cos\Theta$ and $\pm\sin\delta\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)$ cancel, leaving
$$E=\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)\big[\sin\delta\cos\Theta-\sin\Theta\cos\delta+\sin\Theta\big]-\sin\delta\sin\epsilon+\sin\gamma\big[\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)-\sin\beta\big].$$
Since $\sin\delta\cos\Theta-\sin\Theta\cos\delta=\sin(\delta-\Theta)=-\sin\gamma$, the first bracket becomes $\sin\Theta-\sin\gamma$, so
$$E=\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)(\sin\Theta-\sin\gamma)-\sin\delta\sin\epsilon+\sin\gamma(\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)-\sin\beta)$$
$$=\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)\sin\Theta-\sin\delta\sin\epsilon-\sin\gamma\sin\beta.\tag{3}$$

**The key identity.** Using (2), equation (3)$=0$ is equivalent to
$$\sin(\gamma+\epsilon)\sin(\gamma+\delta)=\sin\delta\sin\epsilon+\sin\gamma\sin(\gamma+\delta+\epsilon).\tag{4}$$
By the product-to-sum formula $\sin u\sin v=\tfrac12[\cos(u-v)-\cos(u+v)]$:
- LHS $=\tfrac12[\cos(\epsilon-\delta)-\cos(2\gamma+\epsilon+\delta)]$.
- RHS $=\tfrac12[\cos(\delta-\epsilon)-\cos(\delta+\epsilon)]+\tfrac12[\cos(\delta+\epsilon)-\cos(2\gamma+\delta+\epsilon)]=\tfrac12[\cos(\delta-\epsilon)-\cos(2\gamma+\delta+\epsilon)]$.

Since $\cos(\epsilon-\delta)=\cos(\delta-\epsilon)$, LHS $=$ RHS, so (4) holds and $E=0$.

Therefore $I$ satisfies condition $(\star)$, i.e. the incentre of $\triangle ABD$ lies on line $PQ$. $\blacksquare$

